/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - The surroundings (the environment) are a very large body at temperature T₀ and pressure p₀, usually 25 °C (298 K) or 27 °C (300 K) and 1 atm.
   - A system is at the dead state when it is in full equilibrium with these surroundings: the same temperature T₀ and pressure p₀, at rest (no kinetic energy), at the reference height (no potential energy), and with no chemical difference from 
   - Some syllabi call available energy "availability" and unavailable energy "anergy".
   - Let a reservoir at absolute temperature T give heat Q.
   - AE is the available energy in kJ; Q is the heat given by the source in kJ; T and T₀ are absolute temperatures in K; ΔS = Q/T is the entropy the source gives up, in kJ/K.
   - UE is the unavailable energy in kJ: the heat that even the reversible engine must reject to the surroundings.
   - On a T–s diagram the heat Q is the area under the source line.
   - A real hot body, such as exhaust gas or hot water, cools while it gives heat, so its temperature is not fixed.
   - m is the mass in kg; c is the specific heat in kJ/(kg·K); the body cools from T₁ to T₂, both in K; the heat given is Q = m·c·(T₁ − T₂) in kJ.
   - The most work is obtained when the body cools all the way to T₀; below T₀ it would have to take heat from the surroundings instead.
*/
