/* book: lead: Exergy is the largest useful work a system or a stream can give as it comes to the dead state, exchanging heat only with the surroundings. Energy is always conserved, but exergy is not: every real process destroys some, and the amount destroyed is T₀ times the entropy generated. An exergy balance shows where a plant wastes its ability to do work, and the second law efficiency tells how close a dev */
/* book Figure 4.2.1: The turbine of the first worked example in two accounts: by energy all of the 600 kJ/kg enthalpy drop becomes work, but by exergy the stream gave up 660 kJ/kg, so 60 kJ/kg was destroyed and the second law efficiency is 0.909. */
