/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Take a closed system at a state (T, p) in surroundings at T₀ and p₀.
   - φ is the specific exergy of the closed system in kJ/kg (add V²/2 and gz if it moves or is raised); u, v and s are the specific internal energy (kJ/kg), volume (m³/kg) and entropy (kJ/(kg·K)) at the state; subscript 0 marks the dead state; T
   - u − u₀ is the energy stored above the dead state; p₀(v − v₀) is the work exchanged with the atmosphere as the volume changes to v₀ (negative for a compressed gas); −T₀(s − s₀) is the heat that must pass to or from the surroundings to bring 
   - Between two states the reversible useful work is the fall in exergy, Wrev = Φ₁ − Φ₂, and the exergy destroyed is I = Wrev − Wu.
   - A stream crossing the boundary of a control volume also carries flow work pv, so its exergy uses enthalpy:
   - ψ is the flow exergy in kJ/kg; h is specific enthalpy in kJ/kg; V is velocity in m/s and z height in m (divide V²/2 and gz by 1000 to get kJ/kg).
   - For a steady device, per kg of flow, the exergy that comes in with heat and with the stream equals the work out, the exergy that leaves with the stream, and the exergy destroyed:
   - Σ(1 − T₀/Tk)qk + ψ₁ = w + ψ₂ + i
   - qk is heat received through a part of the boundary at temperature Tk; w is the work out in kJ/kg; i is the exergy destroyed per kg.
   - This is the Gouy-Stodola theorem. sgen is the entropy generated per kg in kJ/(kg·K), found from an entropy balance; for an adiabatic steady device sgen = s₂ − s₁.
*/
